                                "example_id": "a5eb0996-447c-4cb9-a6e0-b2aa4bd23ce5",
                                "sense_id": "ff1717cd-c6cc-3467-9656-e46394a945ec",
                                "text": "2005, Edward Lear, Peter Swaab, Over the land and over the sea\nDancing in the honeymoonlight which bathes the poem, the Owl and the Pussy-cat more wistfully, but less pessimistically, get on terms with another Victorian classic, Matthew Arnold's 'Dover Beach'. Arnold's is also a seaside honeymoon poem, his newlyweds watch 'ignorant armies clash by night' instead of dancing by the light of the moon."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4dc4b658-c1a1-4247-b878-cd44e2ec8b3a",
                                "sense_id": "ff1717cd-c6cc-3467-9656-e46394a945ec",
                                "text": "When her husband joins her / for breakfast, she smiles / the guilty smile of the unfaithful. / Yet does she notice / that the honeymoonlight / has left a few blonde hairs / clinging to his shoulder?"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
